---
/**
 * The emotional center of the page. Written straight, from the Guild's own
 * Case for Support, cut for length. No detail here is invented.
 */
import Section from '@/components/ui/Section.astro';
import Picture from '@/components/ui/Picture.astro';
---

<Section tone="stone" labelledby="story-title">
  <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[2fr_3fr] lg:gap-16">
    <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-8 lg:self-start">
      <Picture slot="foundingStory" sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 35vw, 100vw" aspect="4 / 5" />
      <blockquote class="mt-6 max-w-[40ch] font-serif italic leading-relaxed text-ash">
        <p>
          “All these are skilled with their hands, each one an expert at his own work. Without them
          no city could be lived in. Yet they maintain the fabric of the world.”
        </p>
        <footer class="mt-2 font-sans text-[0.9rem] not-italic">Sirach 38:31, 34</footer>
      </blockquote>
    </div>

    <div class="measure flex flex-col gap-5 font-serif text-[1.125rem] leading-[1.7] md:text-[1.2rem]">
      <h2 id="story-title" class="text-h2 font-sans">
        One man, one van
      </h2>
      <p>
        It began with one man, one plumbing business, and the Lord as his only companion. With
        little more than a budget for small-print classified ads, he spent years alone in his van,
        working in the musty basements of nearly invisible people. There he met the working poor,
        not as customers, but as children of God sitting across from him in their own homes. When it
        came time to settle the bill, he often saw anxiety and embarrassment in their faces. They
        simply could not afford much. So he did what he could: a discount here, a free service
        there. Not because it made business sense, but because it was right.
      </p>
      <p>
        There was one client he never forgot. An elderly woman in a wheelchair, living alone on a
        fixed income in a small, weathered house on the backroads of Missouri. As he replaced her
        toilet, she spoke of her loneliness. No family nearby, no friends to call, no one to turn
        to. When the work was done, she asked him to come back. She could not afford other
        companies. She had no one else to ask. The question stayed with him. If not him, then who?
      </p>
      <p>
        Then a friend from church, newly established in his own blue-collar business, began working
        alongside him. Then another Catholic tradesman joined. Then another. They prayed before and
        after each job. They traded labor, mentored one another, and showed up when a brother or his
        family was in need. What began as one man's quiet attempt to serve became a brotherhood
        bound by faith, work, and a shared calling to serve.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>
